## Step 4: Correlate network and host evidence

The real value appears when one alert leads to the next question. Suppose Suricata flags a request to your web server at 09:15. In the Wazuh dashboard, filter on the server's agent name for the following ten minutes and look for:

- new processes spawned by the web service account;
- file integrity changes in the web root;
- failed or unusual authentication events;
- outbound connections that did not exist before.

A network alert followed by none of these is probably a blocked or irrelevant attempt. A network alert followed by a file change is an incident. Wazuh's own rule groups help here: active response, file integrity monitoring and the Security Configuration Assessment module all add host-side evidence to the same searchable index.

Record what you find in a small table: time, source, signature, host evidence, verdict. That habit turns raw alerts into a defensible triage record, the same skill used in [Incident Response Simulation Lab](/learn/hands-on-security-labs/incident-response-simulation-lab).
